WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.760
A committee, project group, matrix, and self-managing
team are not the same thing.

2
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:10.308
A standing committee addresses ongoing matters; a
temporary committee addresses a bounded need.

3
00:00:10.508 --> 00:00:15.053
Its authority might be advisory or decision-making, so
the assignment must say which.

4
00:00:15.253 --> 00:00:19.249
A project group focuses on a defined result over a
limited period.

5
00:00:19.449 --> 00:00:24.972
A matrix combines overlapping reporting relationships,
such as functional departments and projects.

6
00:00:25.172 --> 00:00:29.366
That can connect expertise, but it can also create
competing priorities.

7
00:00:29.566 --> 00:00:32.914
Adding one committee does not automatically create a
matrix.

8
00:00:33.114 --> 00:00:39.225
A self-managing team takes on responsibilities that
supervisors traditionally handled, within its agreed
scope.

9
00:00:39.425 --> 00:00:42.289
Calling a group a team does not make it self-managing.

10
00:00:42.489 --> 00:00:48.300
For Cedar Crossing's modest workshop problem, clearer
handoffs inside existing departments may be enough.

11
00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:53.490
A temporary project group is another option if the work
needs sustained collaboration.

12
00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:59.387
Compare clarity, workload, service continuity,
flexibility, and coordination cost before choosing.

13
00:00:59.587 --> 00:01:02.106
More structural complexity needs a reason.

